<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00230</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7eb5ad1e-6039-4d73-8416-42cf45fd27af</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">For questions 1 - 4, use the problem and graph below.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
        <quote id="quote-00001">Andre bought a new bag of cat food. The next day, he opened it to feed his cat. The graph shows how many ounces were left in the bag on the days after it was bought. 
            <media alt="Line on coordinate plane, origin O. Horizontal axis from 0 to 44 by 4’s, days since bag was bought. Vertical axis 0 to 64 by 8’s, ounces of cat food. Line passes through 0 comma 56 and 24 comma 16.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_12_10_01.svg"/>
            </media>
          
        </quote>
     </para>
      <exercise id="_5e9c977a-2ed2-4fbc-b91c-ef551070a162" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">How many ounces of food were in the bag 12 days after Andre bought it?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Correct! The answer is 36. When \(x\) is 12, the \(y\) value is 36.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00007">The correct answer is 36. When \(x\) is 12, the \(y\) value is 36.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="ce1fc39f-5784-4486-ace5-f73a9adf1b27" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">How many days did it take for the bag to contain 16 ounces of food?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Correct! The answer is 24. When \(y\) is 16, the\(x\) value is 24.</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00011">The correct answer is 24. When \(y\) is 16, the\(x\) value is 24.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_79e55fd9-7a74-411f-93e3-bef6ec6a184e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">How much did the bag weigh before it was opened?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Correct! The answer is 56. When \(x\) is 0, the \(y\) value is 56.</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00015">The correct answer is 56. When \(x\) is 0, the \(y\) value is 56.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_59199d47-2608-4747-a948-aae74d7989d5" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">Approximately many days did it take for the bag to be empty?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Correct! The answer is 33. When \(y\) is 0, the\(x\) value is 33.</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">The correct answer is 33. When \(y\) is 0, the\(x\) value is 33.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00020">For 5 - 7, use the problem and graph below.</para>
      <para id="para-00021">
        <quote id="quote-00002">A group of hikers is progressing steadily along an uphill trail. The graph shows their elevation (or height above sea level), in feet, at each distance from the start of the trail, in miles.   <!-- REPLACE FIGURE WITH CORRECT IMAGE-->
        
            <media alt=".">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_12_10_01.svg"/>
            </media>
          
        </quote>
     </para>
      <exercise id="f0eeaec7-569a-4f0f-9e9c-47917d747bb2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">What is the slope of the graph?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00023">Correct! The answer is 250. Use points \((0,500)\) and \((2,1000)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00024">\(m=\frac{1000-500}{2-1}=\frac{500}{2}=250\)</para>
          <para id="para-00025">Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00026">The correct answer is \(m=250\). Use points \((0,500)\) and \((2,1000)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00027">\(m=\frac{1000-500}{2-1}=\frac{500}{2}=250\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_5e6b1a80-5a13-4155-a621-7ce662c1d6ab" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00028">What does the slope tell us about this situation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00029">Correct! The answer is the change in elevation as the hikers hike more miles.</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00031">The correct answer is the change in elevation as the hikers hike more miles.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_1274bf04-0581-4f5e-8d9b-2bc79be0c9d5" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00032">Enter the equation that represents the relationship between the hikers' distance from the start of the trail, \(x\), and their elevation, \(y\), using the equation editor.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00033">Correct! The equation is \(y=250x+500\). This equation indicates that for every unit increase in \(x\), the elevation \(y\) increases by 250 units, starting from an initial elevation of 500.</para>
          <para id="para-00034">Try again. Remember to consider how the distance \(x\) affects the elevation \(y\).</para>
          <para id="para-00035">The correct answer is \(y=250x+500\). This equation indicates that for every unit increase in \(x\), the elevation \(y\) increases by 250 units, starting from an initial elevation of 500.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_8c6856b2-7689-47d8-8f42-5e534abb7c33" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00036">A kindergarten teacher bought $21 worth of stickers and cardstock for his class. The stickers cost $1.50 a sheet and the cardstock cost $3.50 per pack. The equation \(1.5s + 3.5c =21\) represents the relationship between sheets of stickers, \(s\), packs of cardstocks, \(c\), and the dollar amount a kindergarten teacher spent on these supplies.</para>
          <para id="para-00037">What does the vertical intercept, (0,6), mean in this situation?</para>
          
            <media alt="Line on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, 0 to 18 by 2’s, sheets of stickers. Vertical axis, 0 to 10 by 2’s, packs of cardstock. Line passes through points 0 comma 6, 7 comma 3, and 14 comma 0.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_12_10_03.svg"/>
            </media>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00038">Correct! The answer is 0 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.</para>
          <para id="para-00039">Try again. Revisit activity 1.10.2.</para>
          <para id="para-00040">The correct answer is 0 sheets of stickers and 6 packs of cardstock were bought.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
